---
import AboutLayout from '../../layouts/AboutLayout.astro';
import '../../styles/about-split.css';

const proofStats = [
  { value: 'Jan. 1, 2023', label: 'Tracking since', note: 'A continuous, evolving structured record' },
  { value: '150+', label: 'Variables tracked', note: 'From sleep and study to hobbies and blunders' },
  { value: '≈ 5 min', label: 'Essential daily logging', note: 'Diary writing is separate and takes longer' },
  { value: '4+', label: 'Core tools', note: 'Sheets, Apps Script, Python, APIs, and whatever earns its place' },
];

const timeline = [
  { year: '2023', title: 'Started tracking', text: 'I began with ordinary daily details: sleep, meals, work, study, routines, and the things memory quietly loses.' },
  { year: '2024', title: 'Connected the system', text: 'The spreadsheets grew into dashboards, formulas, scripts, APIs, and repeatable workflows.' },
  { year: '2025', title: 'Reached diminishing returns', text: 'Adding more data stopped automatically making the system better. The archive had to serve my life rather than consume it.' },
  { year: 'Today', title: 'Turned the private system outward', text: 'LifeLoggerz is becoming a public-safe archive, toolkit, set of explorers, and demonstration of what long-term computer logging can become.' },
];

const systemSteps = [
  { title: 'Record', text: 'Daily logs, diary writing, collections, project notes, and structured variables capture different kinds of evidence.' },
  { title: 'Structure', text: 'Spreadsheets, schemas, formulas, and scripts turn scattered entries into consistent long-term data.' },
  { title: 'Preserve', text: 'The archive keeps context, history, and source material so a chart never becomes the only surviving story.' },
  { title: 'Explore', text: 'Stats, maps, calendars, timelines, search, and eventually AI make the archive useful after the moment of logging has passed.' },
];

const trackedAreas = [
  { icon: '⏱️', title: 'Time & effort', text: 'Work, study, projects, practical tasks, and other blocks of time that show where effort actually went.' },
  { icon: '🌙', title: 'Sleep & routines', text: 'Sleep, wake and bed timing, recurring routines, and the ordinary structure that shapes a day.' },
  { icon: '🎯', title: 'Habits & pursuits', text: 'Exercise, guitar, languages, dance, reading, personal practices, and other skills or habits I return to over time.' },
  { icon: '📚', title: 'Culture & collections', text: 'Books, audiobooks, albums, classical music, art, and the dates, notes, favorites, and context attached to them.' },
  { icon: '🗺️', title: 'Places & experiences', text: 'Travel, outings, social time, notable events, milestones, and experiences that are better remembered with context.' },
  { icon: '📝', title: 'Diary & qualitative context', text: 'Daily events, reflections, questions, mistakes, ideas, and small moments that would lose meaning if reduced to a number.' },
];

const projectCards = [
  { icon: '📊', title: 'Stats & Explore', text: 'Dashboards, records, long-term totals, Day Explorer, and the quantitative side of the archive.', href: '/stats/', cta: 'Explore the data' },
  { icon: '🧰', title: 'Templates', text: 'The practical route in: reusable tools and systems for people who want to try a smaller version themselves.', href: '/templates/', cta: 'Browse templates' },
  { icon: '📝', title: 'Writing & lessons', text: 'What worked, what became excessive, how the system changed, and what the experiments taught me.', href: '/blog/', cta: 'Read the blog' },
  { icon: '🔒', title: 'Privacy & public data', text: 'The structure can be demonstrated publicly without publishing the unrestricted private archive underneath it.', href: '/faq/#privacy-data', cta: 'Read the privacy answers' },
  { icon: '🗂️', title: 'Culture archive', text: 'Books, albums, classical music, art, and other records that make this a life archive rather than a productivity ledger.', href: '/books/', cta: 'Start with Books' },
  { icon: '🛠️', title: 'Build', text: 'Tools, systems, experiments, research, and the growing technical layer behind the public site.', href: '/templates/', cta: 'See the tools' },
];

const structuredData = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'AboutPage',
  name: 'About LifeLoggerz',
  description: 'The origin, philosophy, method, privacy approach, and future of LifeLoggerz.',
  mainEntity: {
    '@type': 'CreativeWork',
    name: 'LifeLoggerz',
    creator: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'José' },
    description: 'A personal data, diary, archive, visualization, and systems project built from long-term daily logging.',
  },
};
---

<AboutLayout
  title="About LifeLoggerz"
  description="What LifeLoggerz is, why I track my life, how the system works, and what I hope other people can take from it."
  pubDate="2026-08-08"
  wide={true}
  showViews={false}
  heroImage="/images_webp/faq-movie.webp"
>
  <main class="about-split">
    <section class="about-hero-copy" aria-labelledby="lifeloggerz-intro-title">
      <p class="about-eyebrow">The archive, system, and philosophy</p>
      <h2 id="lifeloggerz-intro-title">A searchable record of a life in progress</h2>
      <p class="about-lede">
        <strong>LifeLoggerz</strong> began as personal logging and grew into something harder to describe with one label:
        part data system, part diary archive, part memory aid, part experimental laboratory, and now part public toolkit.
      </p>
      <p>
        The purpose is not to turn life into a spreadsheet. It is to preserve more of what happened, make patterns easier to see,
        reduce repeated work, and create an archive that becomes more useful as the years accumulate.
      </p>
      <div class="about-inline-actions">
        <a class="about-button primary" href="/stats/">Explore the System</a>
        <a class="about-button" href="/templates/">Use the Templates</a>
        <a class="about-button" href="/about/me/">Meet the person behind it</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="about-proof-grid" aria-label="LifeLoggerz at a glance">
      {proofStats.map((stat) => (
        <article class="about-proof">
          <strong>{stat.value}</strong>
          <h3>{stat.label}</h3>
          <span>{stat.note}</span>
        </article>
      ))}
    </section>

    <section class="about-section" aria-labelledby="origin-title">
      <div class="about-section-head">
        <p class="about-eyebrow">The origin story</p>
        <h2 id="origin-title">From one spreadsheet to a personal operating system</h2>
        <p>
          I did not begin with a grand architecture. I learned one formula, one dashboard, one script, and one useful automation at a time.
          The system survived because each layer solved a real problem I was already experiencing.
        </p>
      </div>

      <ol class="about-timeline">
        {timeline.map((item) => (
          <li>
            <span class="about-timeline-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <article class="about-timeline-card">
              <p class="about-timeline-year">{item.year}</p>
              <h3>{item.title}</h3>
              <p>{item.text}</p>
            </article>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>

      <div class="about-link-row">
        <a class="about-link-chip" href="/blog/rawdatapost2025/">See how the Raw Data systems evolved →</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="about-section" aria-labelledby="system-title">
      <div class="about-section-head">
        <p class="about-eyebrow">How it works</p>
        <h2 id="system-title">Logging is only the first step</h2>
        <p>The value comes from what happens after an entry is recorded: structure, preservation, retrieval, and exploration.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="about-system-flow">
        {systemSteps.map((step, index) => (
          <article class="about-system-step">
            <span class="about-system-number">Step {index + 1}</span>
            <h3>{step.title}</h3>
            <p>{step.text}</p>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="about-section about-philosophy" aria-labelledby="philosophy-title">
      <div>
        <p class="about-eyebrow">The philosophy</p>
        <h2 id="philosophy-title">Tracking is a tool, not the goal</h2>
        <p>
          More data is not automatically better. I track far more than most people should because the system reflects my own curiosity and has accumulated gradually over years.
        </p>
        <p>
          A good system should reduce mental load, preserve useful memories, answer questions, and improve decisions. When a log creates more friction than clarity, it should be simplified, automated, or removed.
        </p>
      </div>
      <aside class="about-principles" aria-label="LifeLoggerz principles">
        <h3>What I believe now</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>You do not need to track everything.</li>
          <li>Keep only data that earns its place.</li>
          <li>Missing and approximate data should be labeled honestly.</li>
          <li>Automation should remove repetition, not hide understanding.</li>
          <li>The system should make life easier to live, not harder to document.</li>
        </ul>
      </aside>
    </section>

    <section class="about-section" id="what-i-track" aria-labelledby="track-title">
      <div class="about-section-head">
        <p class="about-eyebrow">What I track</p>
        <h2 id="track-title">A life is more than a dashboard</h2>
        <p>
          This is a representative map rather than an exhaustive inventory. LifeLoggerz mixes structured variables with collections, dates, places, and written context so that the archive can preserve both patterns and stories.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="about-card-grid">
        {trackedAreas.map((area) => (
          <article class="about-card">
            <span class="about-card-icon" aria-hidden="true">{area.icon}</span>
            <h3>{area.title}</h3>
            <p>{area.text}</p>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>

      <div class="about-love-panel" style="margin-top: 1rem;">
        <p class="about-eyebrow">Not everything becomes a number</p>
        <h3>Different parts of life deserve different kinds of records.</h3>
        <p style="margin-top: .55rem; color: var(--as-muted);">
          Some things become numbers. Some become dates, maps, timelines, photographs, collections, or written memories. Not everything meaningful needs to become a metric.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="about-section about-philosophy" id="what-i-dont-track" aria-labelledby="dont-track-title">
      <div>
        <p class="about-eyebrow">What I don’t track</p>
        <h2 id="dont-track-title">The archive has edges</h2>
        <p>
          I do not collect a metric simply because it can be measured. Some obvious quantified-self categories are absent, occasional, or deliberately secondary because they do not currently earn enough value for the attention they require.
        </p>
        <p>
          That boundary can change. The point is not to swear off a category forever; it is to avoid turning LifeLoggerz into a contest for maximum data density.
        </p>
      </div>
      <aside class="about-principles" aria-label="What LifeLoggerz deliberately does not prioritize">
        <h3>Not core to the system</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Continuous biometric streams or wearables for their own sake.</li>
          <li>Calories, heart rate, and screen time as default daily targets.</li>
          <li>Perfect minute-by-minute reconstruction of every day.</li>
          <li>Data whose maintenance costs more attention than it returns.</li>
          <li>Private information simply because it would make an interesting chart.</li>
        </ul>
      </aside>
    </section>

    <section class="about-section" aria-labelledby="contribution-title">
      <div class="about-section-head">
        <p class="about-eyebrow">What I hope this contributes</p>
        <h2 id="contribution-title">An ode to tracking and archiving life</h2>
        <p>
          LifeLoggerz is my passion project: an ode to tracking, archiving, and studying one’s own life. My focus is not on building the ultimate biometric dashboard. Calories, heart rate, screen time, wearables, and massive streams of sensor data are their own world, and plenty of people explore that side of quantified life far more deeply than I do.
        </p>
        <p>
          What I care most about is the qualitative side: breaking ordinary days into pieces that can be searched, revisited, compared, and studied later. Diary entries, routines, projects, books, music, art, work, mistakes, questions, and small moments all become part of a record that would otherwise disappear.
        </p>
        <p>
          I built this after being inspired by other people who shared their own systems and experiments online. I want LifeLoggerz to continue that chain. Even if this project seriously inspires one person to preserve more of their life, build a better system, or take an idea here and push it further, I’ll be glad. There is always room to improve, and making the work visible gives someone else a higher place to start from.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <aside class="about-statement" aria-label="LifeLoggerz guiding principle">
      <blockquote>
        “The purpose was never to spend my entire life maintaining a spreadsheet. It was to build systems that returned time and attention to me.”
      </blockquote>
    </aside>

    <section class="about-section" aria-labelledby="contains-title">
      <div class="about-section-head">
        <p class="about-eyebrow">What the public project contains</p>
        <h2 id="contains-title">Several doors into the same archive</h2>
        <p>
          Some visitors want numbers. Some want templates. Some want the philosophy. Others want to wander through books, music, maps, diary ideas, or experiments.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="about-card-grid">
        {projectCards.map((card) => (
          <article class="about-card">
            <span class="about-card-icon" aria-hidden="true">{card.icon}</span>
            <h3>{card.title}</h3>
            <p>{card.text}</p>
            <a class="about-card-link" href={card.href}>{card.cta} →</a>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="about-section" aria-labelledby="computer-title">
      <div class="about-section-head">
        <p class="about-eyebrow">Why computer logging?</p>
        <h2 id="computer-title">A paper journal remembers. A digital archive can also answer.</h2>
        <p>
          Search, statistics, correlations, filtering, timelines, maps, full-corpus phrase lookup, and AI analysis all become possible once records are digital and structured.
          That does not make paper inferior; it explains why a computer-based archive fits what I want to do with years of accumulated material.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="about-card-grid">
        <article class="about-card">
          <span class="about-card-icon" aria-hidden="true">🔎</span>
          <h3>Retrieve</h3>
          <p>Find a date, phrase, person, habit, place, or period without rereading years of notebooks.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="about-card">
          <span class="about-card-icon" aria-hidden="true">📈</span>
          <h3>Compare</h3>
          <p>Turn repeated entries into calendars, distributions, records, trends, and Data Stories.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="about-card">
          <span class="about-card-icon" aria-hidden="true">🤖</span>
          <h3>Analyze</h3>
          <p>Eventually let AI question a scrubbed public diary corpus while keeping the unrestricted private archive private.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="about-section about-final-cta" aria-labelledby="start-title">
      <p class="about-eyebrow">Start much smaller than I did</p>
      <h2 id="start-title">Build a system that fits your life</h2>
      <p>
        Start with one useful thing. Keep what helps. Remove what does not. Let the system grow only when a new layer earns its place.
      </p>
      <div class="about-inline-actions">
        <a class="about-button primary" href="/templates/">Browse Templates</a>
        <a class="about-button" href="/faq/">Read the FAQ</a>
        <a class="about-button" href="mailto:lifeloggerz.official@gmail.com">Contact José</a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <script is:inline type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(structuredData)} />
</AboutLayout>